7.5  Root in a container: users, capabilities and the docker group

TL;DR
  - Without user namespaces, root in a container is root on the host, held back
    only by namespaces, dropped capabilities and seccomp.
  - Being in the docker group equals being root: anyone in it can start a
    container with the host's / mounted and edit any file.
  - Rootless containers (Podman by default, Docker in rootless mode) map
    container root to your own user: an escape gets only your rights.

      id                                  # in the docker group?
      podman run --rm alpine id           # uid=0 inside…
      podman unshare cat /proc/self/uid_map   # …mapped to your uid outside

On your machine
  User      alice (uid 1000, gid 1000)
  Groups    alice, wheel, docker, 4242
  Admin     member of wheel: the group sudo usually trusts
  Umask     0022: new files 644 (rw-r--r--), new folders 755

  Here      no container or virtual machine detected
  Isolated  none: the host's namespaces (pid, user, uts, ipc, cgroup, time)
  Can run   VMs with KVM (/dev/kvm) · containers with Docker
  Overlays  1 overlay mount: container layers, usually

Pitfalls
  - --privileged gives every capability and every device: practically no
    isolation. Add only the capability you need (--cap-add).
  - Mounting /var/run/docker.sock into a container hands it control of the host.
  - Files a rootful container writes to a bind mount belong to root on the host;
    rootless ones get IDs from your range in /etc/subuid.
  - Run the application as a non-root user inside the image (USER in the
    Dockerfile): defence in depth.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 7.5 --why

Related
  Commands  id, podman, docker
  Paths     /etc/subuid, /etc/subgid, /run/docker.sock
  See also  idk 7.2 Namespaces: a private view of the system
  See also  idk 11.4 ACLs and capabilities
  See also  idk 11.3 sudo and groups
